# AiNetCodeNavigator MCP navigation

- Use AiNetCodeNavigator as the primary tool for C# declarations and static relationships when available. Discover exposed tools as needed; use their current schemas for supported arguments, defaults and limits.
- Select the owning target: an absolute existing `.sln` / `.slnx` for source or managed `.dll` / `.exe` for assemblies. Ask only when ownership remains unresolved.
- Read a bounded file range directly when the physical path and line are known. Use focused text searches for literals, configuration, non-C# or unindexed content, unsupported questions, or unavailable/persistently failing navigation. Treat text matches as leads, not semantic proof; state material fallback limitations.
- Request only the context needed for the current question. Reuse discoveries, batch related requests, and start with narrow filters, small pages and shallow traversal. Expand only when needed.
- Pass returned declaration references unchanged with their reported owner target. Do not fabricate references or replace them with display names. Resolve ambiguity by signature, project, file or owner; never choose the first name match.
- Follow response recovery instructions. For running work, wait at least the reported retry interval and poll with the returned `operationToken`; running is not a result. Avoid repeated retries without progress or new evidence.
- Keep the tool, target, query options and page size unchanged when polling or paging; treat tokens as opaque. Read all outer `continuationToken` pages before following a `resultCursor` or body window. Use only the token combination prescribed by the response; change response budgets only as supported.
- Check item/section status, scope, filters, analysis limits and omissions before claiming completeness or absence. A fully delivered page can contain partial analysis; larger response budgets do not expand analysis.
- Base conclusions on the reported snapshot. Do not combine body windows from different snapshots or use stored results as verification of later edits. Rediscover renamed, moved or signature-changed declarations.
- Distinguish static relationships from runtime behavior. Reflection, DI/runtime dispatch and external consumers need separate evidence. Static test candidates do not prove execution or coverage; assembly bodies are decompiled text.
- Use external tools for edits, builds and tests; navigation is read-only. Inspect relevant consumers and runtime wiring before changing shared behavior or public contracts.
